{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- live_context_annotate: Create, resolve, or list durable annotations for exact context sources\n- live_context_recall: Recall a bounded exact source from a durable live-context annotation\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Use live_context_annotate before compacting an exact source that future work must preserve; choose pin only when its exact text must remain visible, continuity for a visible obligation and recall pointer, or archive for recall-only provenance.\n- Use live_context_recall only when an active continuity pointer or archived source is needed; request the smallest useful maxTokens bound.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/nick/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.6.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/AGENTS.md\">\n<!-- redeye:start -->\n## Agent skills\n\n### Issue tracker\n\nIssues are tracked as local markdown files under `.scratch/`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.\n\n### Triage labels\n\nDefault label vocabulary. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.\n\n### Domain docs\n\nSingle-context layout. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.\n<!-- redeye:end -->\n\n## Go conventions\n\n- CI runs `go mod tidy` and fails on a diff, so run it after removing code\n  that was the last user of a dependency.\n- CI runs staticcheck (`go tool -modfile=.github/tools/staticcheck/go.mod\n  staticcheck ./...`); deleting a caller can leave an unused helper that\n  fails U1000.\n- `scripts/licenses.sh` special-cases notices for specific linked packages.\n  When a dependency drops out of the `./cmd/roast` graph, check\n  `go list -deps ./cmd/roast` for each target and update the script.\n- CLI tests drive `ExecuteContext` against an `httptest` fake inference server;\n  `internal/testutil.ClearAmbientEnvironment` strips `ROAST_*`, `OPENAI_*`,\n  `OPENROUTER_*` and `ANTHROPIC_*` in `TestMain`. Config tests reset single\n  variables with `clearProviderEnv` (`internal/config/credential_test.go`);\n  add a new provider's key variable there too.\n## Project config (`internal/config`, `internal/cli/config.go`)\n\n- `config.Load(path)` parses `.roast.json` as JSONC (`hujson.Standardize`,\n  then `encoding/json` with `DisallowUnknownFields`). `version: 1` is required;\n  a literal `api_key` is rejected. There are no built-in roles or models: a\n  role missing from the config fails in `ResolveRole`.\n- Built-in providers (`internal/config/provider.go`): `LookupProvider(name)`\n  and `ProviderAt(endpoint, protocol)` are the one place that says which\n  endpoint, protocols, standard key variable, key page, and login method a\n  provider has. Trust standardness, ambient credentials, and the `init`\n  starter all consult it; add a provider there, not in those callers. A\n  provider's first protocol is the one starter profiles use.\n  `DefaultBaseURL(protocol)` stays separate: it is the implicit endpoint of\n  a profile that omits one; OpenRouter is never one. A role that gets no\n  ambient key reports any set provider key variable as `Withheld`\n  (`withheldKey`), preferring the protocol's default provider.\n- `config.Find(start, stop)` walks up to `stop` (the Git toplevel). It compares\n  directories after `EvalSymlinks` because macOS temp dirs are `/var/...` while\n  `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints `/private/var/...`.\n- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig` / `projectConfigPath`\n  in `internal/cli/config.go`; a missing config is a usage error naming\n  `npx roast init`.\n- CLI tests that run a review need a `.roast.json` (see\n  `internal/cli/project_config_test.go` helpers `projectConfig`, `writeFile`,\n  `chdir`, `newModelRecorder`). Profiles in test configs name a credential\n  with `api_key_env` and set it via `t.Setenv`.\n- Only credentials come from outside the config (`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`, a\n  profile's `api_key_env`, then `OPENAI_API_KEY`/`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, then\n  stored credentials, the last three only at that provider's built-in\n  endpoint). Every other setting is in the config\n  (`limits`, `interactive`, `gate`, `review.snapshot`); optional numbers are\n  pointers so an explicit 0 differs from unset, and `validateSettings` checks\n  ranges. In `cli.go` resolve with `flagOrConfigInt` /\n  `flagOrConfigDuration` (flag, then config, then default).\n- Relative prompt-override paths resolve against the config file's\n  directory: `config.Load` records it and callers read prompts through\n  `cfg.PromptPath(...)`, never the raw `cfg.Prompts.*` string. The trust\n  projection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.\n- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks which\n  file's text reached the fake server must keep decoys out of scope (for\n  example via `.git/info/exclude`).\n- `roast.schema.json` at the repo root is the editor schema for\n  `.roast.json`, shipped through `package.json` `files`. Adding, removing,\n  or retyping a `config.Config` field must update it in the same change;\n  `TestSchemaMatchesConfigStruct` (`internal/config/schema_test.go`) fails\n  on drift either way. The `roast init` starter\n  (`internal/config/starter.go`) shows commented-out defaults that must\n  track the defaults in `internal/cli/cli.go`.\n- The starter places a model by its ID alone (`placeModel`): a\n  `<vendor>/<model>[:<variant>]` ID goes to OpenRouter, a bare ID to the\n  provider `InferFamily` names. A placeholder role follows an OpenRouter\n  role onto OpenRouter; otherwise it takes the other direct provider.\n- `internal/cli/init_test.go` has `providerServer`, a fake speaking the\n  Responses (`/v1/responses`), Anthropic (`/v1/messages`), and OpenRouter\n  chat (`/api/v1/chat/completions`) protocols;\n  pair it with `redirectDefaultTransport(t, server.URL)` to serve profiles\n  on the providers' default endpoints.\n- `internal/cli/settings_test.go` has `requestRecorder` (records model,\n  `Authorization`, body), `settingsRepo`, `reviewMeta` (reads the run's\n  `meta.json`), and `redirectDefaultTransport`, which swaps\n  `http.DefaultTransport` so a profile on a provider's default endpoint can\n  be served by the fake server.\n\n## Trust (`internal/config/trust.go`, `internal/trust`, `internal/cli/trust.go`)\n\n- `Config.TrustProjection()` is the security-relevant projection (ADR-0001);\n  its `Hash()` covers only endpoint, protocol, credential variable, and\n  prompt-override paths. Adding a security-relevant config field means\n  adding it to the projection, or it can change without re-approval.\n- Approvals live in `<git-common-dir>/roast/trust.json`. `roast review`\n  calls `requireTrust` before building scope; untrusted is a usage error\n  (exit 2), never `ExitFindings`.\n- Any CLI test that reviews against the `httptest` server uses a custom\n  endpoint, so it must approve first: `approveProjectConfig(t, ...)` in\n  `trust_test.go` (runs `trust --yes`), or `settingsRepo`, which approves.\n  For a test asserting a config error, approve a valid config and then edit\n  it (`approvedThenEdited`) so a trust failure cannot mask the error.\n- `executeContext(ctx, args, stdin, stdout, stderr)` lets tests answer\n  prompts; `ExecuteContext` passes `os.Stdin`.\n- Terminal detection goes through the package var `interactiveTerminals`\n  (`internal/cli/prompt.go`); tests swap it with `simulateTerminals(t, ...)`\n  (`trust_prompt_test.go`) to exercise the inline trust prompt in `review`.\n- `roast doctor` skips endpoint probes for an unapproved config. A doctor\n  test that probes a custom endpoint must chdir into a temp repo and approve\n  (`approveDoctorConfig` in `doctor_test.go`), never approve in this\n  checkout.\n\n## Stored credentials (`internal/credentials`)\n\n- Project scope is `<git-common-dir>/roast/credentials.json`\n  (`trust.StateDir`, shared with `trust.json`); user scope is\n  `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/roast/credentials.json`, else\n  `~/.config/roast/credentials.json` (ADR-0002).\n- `config` cannot import `credentials` (it validates provider names through\n  `config.LookupProvider`), so `config.StoredCredentials` is an interface.\n  The CLI attaches `credentials.NewStore(\".\")` with `withStoredCredentials`\n  in `internal/cli/config.go`; any new path that resolves roles from a\n  `config.Load` result must attach it too, or stored keys are silently\n  unused. `ResolvedRole` carries the store so `OverrideEndpoint` can rebind.\n- Initial resolution (`resolveCredential`) and override rebinding\n  (`rebindCredential`) must agree: both reach the provider variable and\n  stored key through `ambientCredential`, so a role picks the same\n  credential with or without a same-provider protocol override. Keep\n  `DescribeCredential` in step with any change to that order.\n- Files are read lazily: a bad file fails resolution only when a role\n  reaches it (no env credential first). `roast doctor` also loads each\n  scope's file that exists (`doctorStoredCredentialChecks`, results\n  `project-credentials` / `user-credentials`), so a broken file fails\n  doctor even when env credentials shadow it. Doctor reads the user-scope\n  file too, so doctor tests that expect a clean exit should isolate `HOME`.\n- Tests that touch stored credentials chdir into a temp Git repository (the\n  project file lands in its `.git`) and isolate `HOME` and\n  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` (`isolateCredentialHome` in\n  `internal/cli/stored_credentials_test.go`).\n\n## Login (`internal/cli/login.go`)\n\n- Browser opening goes through the package var `openBrowser`\n  (`internal/cli/prompt.go`, takes a URL). Tests never launch a browser:\n  swap it with `captureBrowser(t)` (`login_test.go`), which returns the\n  URLs it was asked to open. Login prints every URL too, so a failing\n  opener never blocks it.\n- `readSecret` reads without echo only on a real terminal; with test\n  stdin it reads plain lines from one `bufio.Reader` kept across retries,\n  so tests pass several answers as `\"key1\\nkey2\\n\"`.\n- `providerServer` (`init_test.go`) answers `GET /v1/models` for key\n  validation; `answerModels` sets the status per credential. With\n  `redirectDefaultTransport`, login's validation request reaches it.\n  `loginRepo(t)` in `login_test.go` sets up repo, fake, terminal, and\n  opener in one call.\n- `login` with no argument and `init`'s login offer share\n  `providersNeedingLogin` (built-in providers of roles that resolve no\n  credential) and `loginProvider` (one provider, given scope path). A\n  command that prompts and then reads keys must pass one `*bufio.Reader`\n  to both `promptYesDefault` and `loginProvider`; a fresh reader per\n  prompt swallows the buffered answers that tests feed as one string.\n  `initLoginRepo(t, terminals)` in `init_test.go` sets up init tests.\n- OpenRouter login (`openrouter_login.go`) is a PKCE exchange: the fake\n  mints `sk-or-minted-<code>` at `POST /api/v1/auth/keys` and checks keys\n  at `GET /api/v1/key` (`answerKey`). Tests run login in the background\n  (`startRoast`), read the opened URL (`awaitOpened`), and hit its\n  `callback_url` with `callOpenRouterBack`, which uses its own transport\n  because `redirectDefaultTransport` would send loopback requests to the\n  fake. Shorten the callback wait with `shortBrowserTimeout`; any test\n  that might wait for a callback should, so a regression fails fast\n  instead of hanging for five minutes.\n- `logout` (`internal/cli/logout.go`) shares `loginPath` (scope and the\n  outside-a-repository usage error) and `providerNames` with login; keep\n  scope rules in that one helper. Tests seed several providers with\n  `storeKeys` (`logout_test.go`, wraps `credentials.Save`).\n- `storedCredentialRepo` swaps `http.DefaultTransport`, so call it at most\n  once per test or subtest; loop over cases with `t.Run`.\n\n## Agent skill install (`internal/skill`, `internal/cli/skill.go`)\n\n- `skill.InstallationTargets(parent, projectRoot)` resolves targets: a\n  non-empty `parent` (`--dir`) wins, then a non-empty `projectRoot` gives\n  project-local targets, otherwise the user-level targets.\n  `internal/cli/skill.go` maps `--dir`, `--global`, and the Git toplevel onto\n  it; `doctor` inspects user-level targets.\n- The bundled skill text in `internal/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md` describes\n  the install locations; update it when target resolution changes.\n- CLI skill tests isolate `HOME`, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`\n  (see `isolateSkillHome` in `internal/cli/skill_test.go`) so they can assert\n  nothing is written to the real user directories. Do not clear `PATH`: the\n  CLI shells out to `git`.\n## Run records (`internal/record`)\n\n- `record.DefaultDir()` is `.roast/runs` at the Git toplevel of the process\n  working directory (`\"\"` outside a repository); there is no env override.\n  Run directories are `<DefaultDir>/<run-id>/`, the monthly JSONL index is\n  `<DefaultDir>/runs-YYYY-MM.jsonl`.\n- Any test that writes or reads records must `t.Chdir` into a temporary Git\n  repository first (see `useProject` in `internal/record/record_test.go`).\n  Otherwise records land in this checkout's `.roast/`, which ignores itself,\n  so `git status` will not reveal the leak; check with `ls -a`.\n- `roast ignore` (`internal/cli/ignore.go`, `ensureStateIgnored`) owns the\n  toplevel `/.roast/` entry; reuse it rather than writing `.gitignore` again.\n\n## Gate (`internal/gate`, `internal/cli/gate.go`)\n\n- The hook (`internal/gate/pre-commit.sh`, embedded as `HookScript`) runs\n  only `node_modules/.bin/roast` at the Git toplevel, never `PATH`. It first\n  calls the hidden `roast gate settings` (prints `timeout_seconds=` and\n  `severity=` lines from the config's `gate` section, defaults 180/HIGH),\n  then `roast review --staged ... --max-severity <severity>`. Any failure,\n  including a settings query that exits non-zero, warns and allows the\n  commit; only review exit 1 blocks.\n- The hook runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so it sees only a\n  toplevel `.roast.json` (`gateConfigPath` in `internal/cli/config.go`).\n  `gate enable` refuses and the review-time bootstrap stays silent without\n  one; `gate enable` tests must write a toplevel config first.\n- Installed hooks are refreshed by content comparison with `HookScript`, so\n  editing the script needs no version bump.\n- Hook execution tests (`hook_exec_test.go`) drive a real `git commit` with\n  a shell stub that `newStub(t, repo...)` writes at each repo's\n  `node_modules/.bin/roast`; it answers `gate settings` from `STUB_TIMEOUT` /\n  `STUB_SEVERITY` and logs only the review call (`ARGS:` plus the staged\n  diff), so `stub.ran` is empty when no review happened.\n\n## npm packaging (`src/launcher.ts`, `scripts/build.ts`, `packages/`)\n\n- The root `bin` is `dist/launcher.js`, compiled from `src/launcher.ts` by\n  `npm run build:launcher` (`tsc`). It runs\n  `@substrate-system/roast-<platform>-<arch>/dist/roast[.exe]`; the six\n  platform packages live in `packages/` as npm workspaces.\n- `npm run build -- --host` builds only this machine's binary plus the\n  launcher. `npx roast` in this repo resolves the root package's own bin,\n  so build first; a stale or non-executable `dist/launcher.js` lets a\n  `roast` on `PATH` (for example Homebrew) answer instead.\n- `.npmrc` sets `force=true`: npm refuses (EBADPLATFORM) to link workspaces\n  whose `os`/`cpu` do not match the host, even as optional dependencies.\n  Expect \"using --force\" warnings from every npm command here.\n- Launcher tests (`test/*.test.ts`, `node --test`) spawn the compiled\n  launcher from a temp fixture `node_modules` with a shell-script binary,\n  so they need `npm run build:launcher` first; `npm run test:go` does this.\n- Build scripts are TypeScript run directly by Node (type stripping), so\n  they may use only erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter\n  properties.\n- `scripts/platforms.ts` is the single list of the six targets and their\n  package names/paths; `build.ts`, `version.ts` (`version` lifecycle),\n  `check-release.ts` (`prepublishOnly`) and `release.ts` share it. All but\n  `build.ts` operate on `process.cwd()` so tests can run them in a fixture.\n- Release-script tests (`test/release-fixture.ts`) build a temp package\n  with the real root `scripts` and a symlink to `scripts/`, then run\n  `npm version` / `npm run prepublishOnly` there; `release.test.ts` puts a\n  logging fake `npm` first on `PATH`. None of them compile Go.\n- `npm run check:pack` (`scripts/check-pack.ts`, CI only) dry-run packs\n  every package and checks each tarball lists its `dist/` contents; changing\n  a package's `files` or what `build.ts` writes into `dist/` must keep it\n  passing.\n  It also requires Roast's own license files (`LICENSES` in\n  `scripts/platforms.ts`, copied into each platform `dist/` by `build.ts`)\n  and a `license` field in every manifest.\n- `npm run release` runs `scripts/check-release.ts` itself, with Node, after\n  the build and before `npm publish --workspaces`: platform packages have no\n  `prepublishOnly`, so the root hook alone would run too late.\n- The root package has no `dependencies`; the launcher uses only Node\n  built-ins, and anything listed there installs into every user's project. npm 12 prints `npm pack --json` as an object keyed by package\n  name, older npm as an array.\n- There is no committed lockfile (`.npmrc` sets `package-lock=false`). CI\n  writes one with `npm install --package-lock-only --package-lock=true`\n  before `npm ci`, which refuses to run without it.\n- CI's test step runs `npm run test:go` with `GOFLAGS=-race`, so Go tests that\n  spawn `go` subprocesses inherit `-race`; such a subprocess must not also\n  set `CGO_ENABLED=0` for a build.\n- A full `npm run build` takes several minutes: it runs\n  `scripts/licenses.sh` once per target and copies the resulting root\n  `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/` into each package's `dist/`.\n\n## TypeScript rewrite tooling (M0 to M5)\n\n- `npm run test:ts` bundles `dist/cli.js` (`npm run bundle`), bundles\n  `test/index.ts` to `test/test-bundle.js` (`build:test`), and pipes it to\n  `tap-spec` (`test:tap`). A new test module must be imported from\n  `test/index.ts`. `npm test` runs `typecheck` and then this TS suite\n  (`test:ts` is an alias); esbuild drops unused imports without\n  resolving them, so only typecheck catches a broken import.\n  `npm run test:go` runs the Go and launcher tests.\n- `scripts/bundle.ts` holds the esbuild options for every bundle (text\n  loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`; `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`;\n  the `require` shim banner). A test that bundles code itself must use\n  the same loaders and banner.\n- Lint runs eslint through `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, a resolve hook that\n  gives the eslint process the TypeScript 6 API\n  (`@typescript/typescript6`), because typescript-eslint throws on the\n  TypeScript 7 package that `typecheck` uses. Run lint with\n  `npm run lint`, not a bare `eslint`.\n- Legacy launcher and release files are excluded from lint and typecheck\n  by name (`lint` `--ignore-pattern` list, `tsconfig.json` `exclude`); the\n  launcher compiles with `tsconfig.launcher.json`.\n- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing\n  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers must sit in other modules.\n- `npm run bundle` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt` through\n  `scripts/licenses.ts` and fails, naming the package, when a bundled\n  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new\n  runtime dependency must pass that check; extending\n  `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs maintainer approval.\n  `@substrate-system/debug` currently fails it, so no `src/` module may\n  import it yet.\n- `scripts/check-package.ts` is the post-M6 package check. It fails\n  against today's transitional manifest, so only its fixture tests\n  (`test/tooling/check-package.ts`) run it; do not wire it into CI or\n  npm scripts before M6.\n- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`, `run`).\n  Shell hooks here block heredocs that mention `process.env.PATH`; write\n  such files with an editor tool.\n\n- Shared TS helpers (import these, never re-implement): `src/gocompat/`\n  (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/byte-order sort and\n  `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as bigint nanoseconds;\n  `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`), `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`,\n  `src/fsutil/atomic.ts` (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust,\n  credentials, and gate temp names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts`\n  (the only git runner; pass the CLI context's `env`, the runner reads\n  `process.env` only when none is given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).\n- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script\n  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through\n  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.\n- TS CLI core (`src/cli/`): commands are `CommandDef`s\n  (`src/cli/command.ts`) listed in `src/cli/commands.ts`; `run.ts`\n  builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand` (tests),\n  and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds. A handler gets a\n  `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared `LineReader`) and\n  throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; never writes it or\n  reads process globals. A group with `subcommands` and no handler\n  prints its help.\n- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through\n  `terminalHooks` (`src/cli/terminal.ts`); tests use `simulateTerminals`\n  / `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`) with the `test`\n  wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style `t.cleanup`\n  (tapzero has no teardown hook).\n- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`); it\n  makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:\n  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).\n  `test/cli/process.ts` spawns `dist/cli.js`, so it needs a fresh\n  `npm run bundle` (test:ts does that).\n- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the\n  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command\n  registers.\n- eslint here rejects control characters in regex literals\n  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char\n  codes and use helper functions instead.\n- Keep non-ASCII out of TS source: write `\\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing\n  tools decode such escapes into literal characters when writing; check\n  with `grep -nP '[^\\x00-\\x7f]'` after writing a file.\n- Scope (`src/scope/`, public surface in `index.ts`): every `git diff`\n  argument list starts with `DIFF_CONFIG` (use `diffArgs(...)` in\n  `src/scope/git.ts`); git rejects an empty `diff.orderFile=`, so it is\n  `/dev/null`. Patch output goes through `gitPatch` (streams into a\n  `SectionCapper`), never the buffered runner. `build` takes the git\n  environment as `BuildOpts.env` (the CLI context's env).\n- The secret denylist lives once in `src/snapshot/denylist.ts`; scope,\n  context, and snapshot import it.\n- Scope tests use `newRepo()` (`test/scope/helpers.ts`): a temp repo with\n  its own HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME, and `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` file\n  (`repo.globalConfig`, safe to fill with hostile config), passed to\n  `build` as `env:repo.env`. APFS refuses non-UTF-8 file names; stage such\n  a name with `git update-index --index-info` to test it on macOS.\n- Memory bounds: plain RSS growth reflects V8 GC timing on discarded pipe\n  chunks, not what code keeps alive. `test/scope/huge.ts` forces gc\n  (`v8.setFlagsFromString('--expose-gc')`