{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let's start by understanding the task. This is ticket 31: the `login` paste flow, part 1 — storing keys. I need to do the following:\n\n1. First, use the /tdd skill (the instructions say \"use /tdd to implement the ticket\").\n2. Before writing any code, read the files directly related to the ticket.\n3. Work only on this ticket.\n4. Keep notes in `.redeye/notes.md`.\n5. Commit after each checklist item.\n6. Run the full `npm test` at the end.\n\nFirst, let me load the tdd skill, and then investigate the repository structure.\n\nTicket contents:\n- `npx roast login <provider>` for storing the provider's API key (default is project scope, `--global` is user scope)\n- After opening the provider's key page (skipped with `--no-browser`), paste the key at a no-echo prompt\n- Before storing, verify the key against the provider's `GET /v1/models`\n- Never display the key\n- Targets are OpenAI, Anthropic (paste providers)\n- Shared `loginPath` (scope rules + outside-repo usage error) and `providerNames`\n- Verification uses a swappable `fetch` with `redirect: 'manual'`, and the body is read up to a max of 32 KiB\n- Test fakes are in `test/helpers/provider-server.ts`\n- `test/cli/login.ts`\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n1. `login anthropic` with a pasted key (trimming surrounding whitespace) is saved to the project's `credentials.json` (0600 inside a 0700 directory), and a subsequent review to the built-in endpoint sends it.\n2. `login openai --global` is saved only to the user file, and the verification request includes `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.\n3. The key page URL is passed to `openBrowser` (except in the case of `--no-browser`). A failing opener must not cause login to fail.\n4. Logging in again replaces the saved key.\n5. No portion of any pasted key must appear in stdout, and stdout must not be empty.\n6. `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run test:ts` pass, and the Go build and Go tests also pass.\n\nFiles to touch:\n- `src/cli/login.ts` (login command, `runLogin`, `loginPath`, `providerNames`, `pasteLogin`, `validateKey`, `keyCheckPath`)\n- `src/cli/terminal.ts` (raw-mode `readSecret`, if not present)\n- `test/helpers/provider-server.ts` (shared fake provider server, also used by the `init` tests)\n- `test/cli/login.ts`\n\nWait a sec — the ticket says `test/helpers/provider-server.ts`, but AGENTS.md says the shared helpers include `test/helpers/spawn.ts` and so on. Let me check what exists. Also, the failed attempt 4 log had an error in `test/cli/login.ts` — a type error where the `provider-server` helper was returning a Promise. That means a previous attempt created `test/helpers/provider-server.ts` that returns a Promise, and login.ts expected a synchronous `ProviderServer`. This time I need to do it correctly.\n\nLet me start by reading the tdd skill and exploring the repository first.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"t3zJbvmsRNUSvwOzBsNhqXBKRXm1cW9p","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/nick/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"XxKG9eVi9mNJ9BADilKrjEuwKB6thbU9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste && ls /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/src /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/test /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/test/helpers /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/test/cli /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/spec 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"tiiny","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B","usage":{"input":26796,"output":915,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":27711,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1795227430828,"responseId":"chatcmpl-gNWo0agYX35xCznUUWhdztkhHL53iRLA","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","durationMs":122378,"thinkingLevel":"minimal"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"t3zJbvmsRNUSvwOzBsNhqXBKRXm1cW9p","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: tdd\ndescription: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests.\n---\n\n# Test-Driven Development\n\nTDD is the red → green loop. This skill is the reference that makes that loop produce tests worth keeping: what a good test is, where tests go, the anti-patterns, and the rules of the loop. Every section applies on every cycle: consult them before and during the loop, not after.\n\nWhen exploring the codebase, read `CONTEXT.md` (if it exists) so test names and interface vocabulary match the project's domain language, and respect ADRs in the area you're touching.\n\n## What a good test is\n\nTests verify behavior through public interfaces, not implementation details. Code can change entirely; tests shouldn't. A good test reads like a specification: \"user can checkout with valid cart\" tells you exactly what capability exists, and it survives refactors because it doesn't care about internal structure.\n\nSee [tests.md](tests.md) for examples and [mocking.md](mocking.md) for mocking guidelines.\n\n## Seams: where tests go\n\nA **seam** is the public boundary you test at: the interface where you observe behavior without reaching inside. Tests live at seams, never against internals.\n\n**Test only at pre-agreed seams.** Before writing any test, write down the seams under test and confirm them with the user. No test is written at an unconfirmed seam. You can't test everything, so agreeing the seams up front is how testing effort lands on the critical paths and complex logic instead of every edge case.\n\nAsk: \"What's the public interface, and which seams should we test?\"\n\nWhen the shape of that interface is itself in question (how deep the module is, where the seam belongs, what the interface should expose), call the Skill tool with \"codebase-design\" for the vocabulary. It is the shared source of the module, interface, depth, seam, adapter, leverage and locality terms, and it is a reference to consult, not a session to run.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- **Implementation-coupled**: mocks internal collaborators, tests private methods, or verifies through a side channel (querying the database instead of using the interface). The tell: the test breaks when you refactor but behavior hasn't changed.\n- **Tautological**: the assertion recomputes the expected value the way the code does (`expect(add(a, b)).toBe(a + b)`, a snapshot derived by hand the same way, a constant asserted equal to itself), so it passes by construction and can never disagree with the code. Expected values must come from an independent source of truth: a known-good literal, a worked example, the spec.\n- **Horizontal slicing**: writing all tests first, then all implementation. Bulk tests verify _imagined_ behavior: you test the _shape_ of things rather than user-facing behavior, the tests go insensitive to real changes, and you commit to test structure before understanding the implementation. Work in **vertical slices** instead: one test → one implementation → repeat, each test a **tracer bullet** that responds to what the last cycle taught you.\n\n## Rules of the loop\n\n- **Red before green.** Write the failing test first, then only enough code to pass it. Don't anticipate future tests or add speculative features.\n- **One slice at a time.** One seam, one test, one minimal implementation per cycle.\n- **Refactoring is not part of the loop.** It belongs to the review stage (see the `code-review` skill), not the red → green implementation cycle."}],"isError":false,"durationMs":8,"timestamp":1795227553219},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"XxKG9eVi9mNJ9BADilKrjEuwKB6thbU9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AGENTS.md\nCHANGELOG.md\ncmd\nCONTEXT.md\nCONTRIBUTING.md\ndocs\neslint.confi