{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":8900,"output":1474,"cacheRead":31828,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":42202,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Okay, now I understand the spec. This ticket covers **part 1**: the paste flow for storing keys. Specifically:\n- `runLogin` (with provider argument), `loginPath`, `providerNames`, `pasteLogin`, `validateKey`, `keyCheckPath` in `src/cli/login.ts`\n- `readSecret` in `src/cli/terminal.ts` (raw mode, if it doesn't exist yet)\n- `test/helpers/provider-server.ts` (shared fake)\n- `test/cli/login.ts`\n\nThe ticket's acceptance criteria (part 1 only — paste login):\n1. `login anthropic` with a pasted key (trimmed) gets saved to the project's credentials.json (0600/0700), and a subsequent review to the built-in endpoint sends it.\n2. `login openai --global` saves only to the user file, and the validation request includes `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.\n3. The key page URL is passed to `openBrowser` (except when `--no-browser` is specified). A failing opener does not cause login to fail.\n4. Logging in again replaces the saved key.\n5. No portion of any pasted key appears in stdout, and stdout is non-empty.\n6. `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run test:ts` pass, and Go build + Go tests also pass.\n\nThe spec test plan for paste login (section 5) includes more tests than the acceptance criteria:\n- `TestLoginStoresAPastedKeyForTheProject` (pasted key is saved for the project + a subsequent review sends it) ✓ AC1\n- `TestLoginGlobalStoresTheKeyForTheUserOnly` (--global saves only to user file; Bearer header) ✓ AC2\n- `TestLoginOpensTheKeyPageUnlessNoBrowser` (AC3a)\n- `TestLoginSucceedsWhenTheBrowserCannotOpen` (AC3b)\n- `TestLoginAsksAgainAfterARejectedKey` (retry)\n- `TestLoginStoresNothingAfterThreeRejectedKeys` (3 rejections → exit 3, no file)\n- `TestLoginStoresNothingWhenTheKeyCannotBeChecked` (5xx / network failure → exit 4, no file)\n- `TestLoginAgainReplacesTheStoredKey` (AC4)\n- `TestLoginUsageErrors` (unknown provider / no terminal / outside a repo → exit 2)\n- `TestLoginNeverPrintsTheKeyToStdout` (AC5)\n\nThe ticket says: \"Redirects, retry, and cancellation tests are ticket 41. No-argument `login`, `loginProvider`, and `logout` are ticket 42.\"\n\nHmm, wait — \"loginProvider is ticket 42\"? Let me reread: \"No-argument `login`, `loginProvider`, and `logout` are ticket 42.\" This is odd, because `loginProvider` is needed for paste login… I think this means \"no-argument login (`runLoginMissing`), the OpenRouter browser login flow (`loginProvider`'s browser branch), and `logout` are ticket 42.\" The `loginProvider` dispatch is needed for this ticket: `loginProvider(provider, flags, target, ctx)` → paste → `pasteLogin`. The dispatch exists but the `browser` branch becomes a placeholder (\"login to openrouter is not supported...\" until ticket 32 — actually the spec says openrouter is ticket 32). The previous attempt's notes said: \"loginProvider (paste dispatch; browser methods are placeholder errors until ticket 32; saveCredential + stored line to stdout)\".\n\nSo for this ticket, when a user runs `login openrouter`, the browser branch should error out. What kind of error? The spec 2.6 says: \"Dispatch on `provider.login`: `paste` → paste flow (2.7); `browser` → OpenRouter flow (2.8); otherwise → plain error `login to <name> is not supported; set <keyEnv>` (unreachable in the current registry).\" Hmm, in the current registry, openrouter's login is `browser`, so during this ticket, `login openrouter` will… the ticket only covers paste providers (OpenAI, Anthropic). So what should `login openrouter` do during this ticket? The previous attempt made the browser method a \"placeholder error until ticket 32.\" Let me think about what's most reasonable: since ticket 32 (openrouter-pkce-login) adds the browser flow, for now `loginProvider` can treat `browser` as unsupported → plain error `login to <name> is not supported; set <keyEnv>`. That's the \"otherwise\" branch. This is consistent with Go behavior only after ticket 32 is complete; during the transition, the error is reasonable, and it's a placeholder error the user sees. The previous attempt did exactly that. I'll follow that.\n\nNow, let me read the existing code:\n\n1. `src/cli/terminal.ts` — LineReader, readSecret?, openBrowser, interactiveTerminals, promptYesDefault\n2. `src/cli/config.ts` — loadProjectConfig, NoProjectConfigError\n3. `src/cli/command.ts` + `commands.ts` + `run.ts` — command registration\n4. `src/cli/errors.ts`\n5. `src/credentials/credentials.ts`\n6. `src/config/provider.ts`\n7. `src/llm/errors.ts`, `src/llm/http.ts`\n8. `test/helpers/terminal.ts`, `test/helpers/test.ts`, `test/helpers/run.ts`, `test/helpers/repo.ts`, `test/helpers/env.ts`, `test/helpers/inference-server.ts`, `test/helpers/inference-stream.ts`\n9. `test/cli/stored-keys.ts` (as a model for CLI tests), `test/cli/terminal.ts` (readSecret tests, if they exist)\n10. Go's `internal/cli/login.go`, `internal/cli/prompt.go`, `internal/cli/init_test.go` (providerServer), `internal/cli/settings_test.go` (redirectDefaultTransport), `internal/cli/login_test.go`.\n\nLet me start by reading the TS files.\n"}}
