{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Okay, now I understand the spec. This ticket covers **part 1**: the paste flow for storing keys. Specifically:\n- `runLogin` (with provider argument), `loginPath`, `providerNames`, `pasteLogin`, `validateKey`, `keyCheckPath` in `src/cli/login.ts`\n- `readSecret` in `src/cli/terminal.ts` (raw mode, if it doesn't exist yet)\n- `test/helpers/provider-server.ts` (shared fake)\n- `test/cli/login.ts`\n\nThe ticket's acceptance criteria (part 1 only — paste login):\n1. `login anthropic` with a pasted key (trimmed) gets saved to the project's credentials.json (0600/0700), and a subsequent review to the built-in endpoint sends it.\n2. `login openai --global` saves only to the user file, and the validation request includes `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.\n3. The key page URL is passed to `openBrowser` (except when `--no-browser` is specified). A failing opener does not cause login to fail.\n4. Logging in again replaces the saved key.\n5. No portion of any pasted key appears in stdout, and stdout is non-empty.\n6. `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run test:ts` pass, and Go build + Go tests also pass.\n\nThe spec test plan for paste login (section 5) includes more tests than the acceptance criteria:\n- `TestLoginStoresAPastedKeyForTheProject` (pasted key is saved for the project + a subsequent review sends it) ✓ AC1\n- `TestLoginGlobalStoresTheKeyForTheUserOnly` (--global saves only to user file; Bearer header) ✓ AC2\n- `TestLoginOpensTheKeyPageUnlessNoBrowser` (AC3a)\n- `TestLoginSucceedsWhenTheBrowserCannotOpen` (AC3b)\n- `TestLoginAsksAgainAfterARejectedKey` (retry)\n- `TestLoginStoresNothingAfterThreeRejectedKeys` (3 rejections → exit 3, no file)\n- `TestLoginStoresNothingWhenTheKeyCannotBeChecked` (5xx / network failure → exit 4, no file)\n- `TestLoginAgainReplacesTheStoredKey` (AC4)\n- `TestLoginUsageErrors` (unknown provider / no terminal / outside a repo → exit 2)\n- `TestLoginNeverPrintsTheKeyToStdout` (AC5)\n\nThe ticket says: \"Redirects, retry, and cancellation tests are ticket 41. No-argument `login`, `loginProvider`, and `logout` are ticket 42.\"\n\nHmm, wait — \"loginProvider is ticket 42\"? Let me reread: \"No-argument `login`, `loginProvider`, and `logout` are ticket 42.\" This is odd, because `loginProvider` is needed for paste login… I think this means \"no-argument login (`runLoginMissing`), the OpenRouter browser login flow (`loginProvider`'s browser branch), and `logout` are ticket 42.\" The `loginProvider` dispatch is needed for this ticket: `loginProvider(provider, flags, target, ctx)` → paste → `pasteLogin`. The dispatch exists but the `browser` branch becomes a placeholder (\"login to openrouter is not supported...\" until ticket 32 — actually the spec says openrouter is ticket 32). The previous attempt's notes said: \"loginProvider (paste dispatch; browser methods are placeholder errors until ticket 32; saveCredential + stored line to stdout)\".\n\nSo for this ticket, when a user runs `login openrouter`, the browser branch should error out. What kind of error? The spec 2.6 says: \"Dispatch on `provider.login`: `paste` → paste flow (2.7); `browser` → OpenRouter flow (2.8); otherwise → plain error `login to <name> is not supported; set <keyEnv>` (unreachable in the current registry).\" Hmm, in the current registry, openrouter's login is `browser`, so during this ticket, `login openrouter` will… the ticket only covers paste providers (OpenAI, Anthropic). So what should `login openrouter` do during this ticket? The previous attempt made the browser method a \"placeholder error until ticket 32.\" Let me think about what's most reasonable: since ticket 32 (openrouter-pkce-login) adds the browser flow, for now `loginProvider` can treat `browser` as unsupported → plain error `login to <name> is not supported; set <keyEnv>`. That's the \"otherwise\" branch. This is consistent with Go behavior only after ticket 32 is complete; during the transition, the error is reasonable, and it's a placeholder error the user sees. The previous attempt did exactly that. I'll follow that.\n\nNow, let me read the existing code:\n\n1. `src/cli/terminal.ts` — LineReader, readSecret?, openBrowser, interactiveTerminals, promptYesDefault\n2. `src/cli/config.ts` — loadProjectConfig, NoProjectConfigError\n3. `src/cli/command.ts` + `commands.ts` + `run.ts` — command registration\n4. `src/cli/errors.ts`\n5. `src/credentials/credentials.ts`\n6. `src/config/provider.ts`\n7. `src/llm/errors.ts`, `src/llm/http.ts`\n8. `test/helpers/terminal.ts`, `test/helpers/test.ts`, `test/helpers/run.ts`, `test/helpers/repo.ts`, `test/helpers/env.ts`, `test/helpers/inference-server.ts`, `test/helpers/inference-stream.ts`\n9. `test/cli/stored-keys.ts` (as a model for CLI tests), `test/cli/terminal.ts` (readSecret tests, if they exist)\n10. Go's `internal/cli/login.go`, `internal/cli/prompt.go`, `internal/cli/init_test.go` (providerServer), `internal/cli/settings_test.go` (redirectDefaultTransport), `internal/cli/login_test.go`.\n\nLet me start by reading the TS files.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me read the relevant source files in parallel.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"lyL0JYHS3ZyjR9DL3KqtPzw2UsLxxGGQ","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/src/cli/terminal.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"57DRnyhUeLtgXZvbRbQsOhzdiLMn912I","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/src/cli/command.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"4p274PvIeLHllBaEX9PkuO2lnh5cvXgi","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-31-login-paste/src/cli/commands.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"tiiny","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B","usage":{"input":8900,"output":1474,"cacheRead":31828,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":42202,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1795227642296,"responseId":"chatcmpl-F6nlYV6Ukb1aonS5kNfWwEwSsNUSgSql","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","durationMs":124450,"thinkingLevel":"minimal"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"lyL0JYHS3ZyjR9DL3KqtPzw2UsLxxGGQ","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"// Shared terminal helpers (spec 01, 2.16): yes/no prompt, secret input,\n// terminal detection, browser opening. Terminal detection and browser\n// opening are swappable through `terminalHooks` so tests never touch a\n// real terminal or launch a browser.\nimport { spawn } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { isatty } from 'node:tty'\nimport { goTrimSpace } from '../gocompat/strings.js'\nimport { CancelledError } from './errors.js'\n\n// Reads a stream one character or line at a time. One reader serves every\n// prompt in a command, so input buffered past one answer is kept for the\n// next. The stream is attached lazily, on the first read.\nexport class LineReader {\n    private readonly stream:NodeJS.ReadableStream\n    private buf = ''\n    private ended = false\n    private error:Error|undefined\n    private attached = false\n    private wake:(() => void)|undefined\n\n    constructor (stream:NodeJS.ReadableStream) {\n        this.stream = stream\n    }\n\n    private attach ():void {\n        if (this.attached) return\n        this.attached = true\n        const notify = ():void => {\n            const w = this.wake\n            this.wake = undefined\n            w?.()\n        }\n        this.stream.setEncoding('utf8')\n        this.stream.on('data', (chunk:string) => {\n            this.buf += chunk\n            notify()\n        })\n        this.stream.on('end', () => {\n            this.ended = true\n            notify()\n        })\n        this.stream.on('error', (err:Error) => {\n            this.error = err\n            notify()\n        })\n    }\n\n    // Waits until the buffer holds data, the stream ended, or failed.\n    private async fill ():Promise<void> {\n        this.attach()\n        while (this.buf === '' && !this.ended && !this.error) {\n            this.stream.resume()\n            await new Promise<void>(resolve => { this.wake = resolve })\n        }\n        this.stream.pause()\n        if (this.buf === '' && this.error) throw this.error\n    }\n\n    // The next character, or null at end of input.\n    async readChar ():Promise<string|null> {\n        await this.fill()\n        if (this.buf === '') return null\n        const c = String.fromCodePoint(this.buf.codePointAt(0) as number)\n        this.buf = this.buf.slice(c.length)\n        return c\n    }\n\n    // The next line without its line ending, or null at end of input with\n    // nothing read. A last line without a newline is returned as is.\n    async readLine ():Promise<string|null> {\n        let line = ''\n        for (;;) {\n            await this.fill()\n            if (this.buf === '') return line === '' ? null : line\n            const i = this.buf.indexOf('\\n')\n            if (i >= 0) {\n                line += this.buf.slice(0, i)\n                this.buf = this.buf.slice(i + 1)\n                return line.endsWith('\\r') ? line.slice(0, -1) : line\n            }\n            line += this.buf\n            this.buf = ''\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Writes `<question> [Y/n] ` and reads one answer: empty, y, or yes (any\n// case) is true; anything else, or end of input, is false.\nexport async function promptYesDefault (\n    out:NodeJS.WritableStream,\n    lines:LineReader,\n    question:string\n):Promise<boolean> {\n    out.write(`${question} [Y/n] `)\n    const answer = await lines.readLine()\n    if (answer === null) return false\n    const a = goTrimSpace(answer).toLowerCase()\n    return a === '' || a === 'y' || a === 'yes'\n}\n\n// Writes `<question> [y/N] ` and reads one answer: only y or yes (any\n// case, surrounding space ignored) is true; empty input, anything else,\n// or end of input is false.\nexport async function promptNoDefault (\n    out:NodeJS.WritableStream,\n    lines:LineReader,\n    question:string\n):Promise<boolean> {\n    out.write(`${question} [y/N] `)\n    const answer = await lines.readLine()\n    if (answer === null) return false\n    const a = goTrimSpace(answer).toLowerCase()\n    return a === 'y' || a === 'yes'\n}\n\ninterface RawTTY {\n    isTTY?:boolean;\n    setRawMode?:(on:boolean) => unknown;\n}\n\nfunction rawCapable (stdin:NodeJS.ReadableStream):stdin is\n    NodeJS.ReadableStream&Required<RawTTY> {\n    const s = stdin as NodeJS.ReadableStream&RawTTY\n    return s.isTTY === true && typeof s.setRawMode === 'function'\n}\n\nconst CTRL_C = '\\x03'\nconst CTRL_D = '\\x04'\nconst BACKSPACE = '\\x08'\nconst DELETE = '\\x7f'\n\n// Reads one line in raw mode without echo. Ctrl-C cancels.\nasync function readRawLine (lines:LineReader):Promise<string|null> {\n    let line = ''\n    for (;;) {\n        const c = await lines.readChar()\n        if (c === null) return line === '' ? null : line\n        if (c === '\\r' || c === '\\n') return line\n        if (c === CTRL_C) throw new CancelledError()\n        if (c === CTRL_D && line === '') return null\n        if (c === BACKSPACE || c === DELETE) {\n            line = line.slice(0, -1)\n            continue\n        }\n        line += c\n    }\n}\n\n// Writes `prompt` and reads one secret. On a TTY it reads in raw mode\n// without echo (Ctrl-C is cancellation); otherwise one plain line from\n// the shared reader. The result is trimmed; end of input with no text is\n// an error.\nexport async function readSecret (\n    out:NodeJS.WritableStream,\n    stdin:NodeJS.ReadableStream,\n    lines:LineReader,\n    prompt:string\n):Promise<string> {\n    out.write(prompt)\n    let answer:string|null\n    if (rawCapable(stdin)) {\n        stdin.setRawMode(true)\n        try {\n            answer = await readRawLine(lines)\n        } finally {\n            stdin.setRawMode(false)\n            out.write('\\n')\n        }\n    } else {\n        answer = await lines.readLine()\n    }\n    if (answer === null) throw new Error('no input: end of file')\n    return goTrimSpace(answer)\n}\n\nfunction isTerminal (stream:unknown):boolean {\n    const s = stream as { isTTY?:boolean, fd?:unknown }\n    return s.isTTY === true && typeof s.fd === 'number' && isatty(s.fd)\n}\n\n// Both streams are real terminals. A stream without a file descriptor,\n// such as a test buffer, never is.\nfunction defaultInteractiveTerminals (\n    stdin:NodeJS.ReadableStream,\n    stderr:NodeJS.WritableStream\n):boolean {\n    return isTerminal(stdin) && isTerminal(stderr)\n}\n\n// Starts `open <url>` (darwin) or `xdg-open <url>` without waiting for\n// it. Resolves with a spawn error, or undefined once started. The child\n// is killed when `signal` aborts.\nfunction defaultOpenBrowser (\n    url:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal\n):Promise<Error|undefined> {\n    const cmd = process.platform === 'darwin' ? 'open' : 'xdg-open'\n    return new Promise(resolve => {\n        const child = spawn(cmd, [url], {\n            stdio:'ignore',\n            detached:true,\n            signal,\n        })\n        child.on('spawn', () => {\n            child.unref()\n            resolve(undefined)\n        })\n        // Also swallows the AbortError emitted when `signal` kills it.\n        child.on('error', err => resolve(err))\n    })\n}\n\nexport const terminalHooks = {\n    interactiveTerminals:defaultInteractiveTerminals,\n    openBrowser:defaultOpenBrowser,\n}\n\nexport function interactiveTerminals (\n    stdin:NodeJS.ReadableStream,\n    stderr:NodeJS.WritableStream\n):boolean {\n    return terminalHooks.interactiveTerminals(stdin, stderr)\n}\n\nexport function openBrowser (\n    url:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal\n):Promise<Error|undefined> {\n    return terminalHooks.openBrowser(url, signal)\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":7,"timestamp":1795227766755},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"57DRnyhUeLtgXZvbRbQsOhzdiLMn912I","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"// The command registration API. Area tickets describe their commands as\n// CommandDefs and list them in src/cli/commands.ts; tests add their own\n// with registerCommand. run.ts builds the yargs tree from both.\nimport type { Argv, ArgumentsCamelCase } from 'yargs'\nimport type { ErrorFormat } from './errors.js'\nimport type { LineReader } from './terminal.js'\n\n// Everything a command handler may touch. Handlers never read process\n// globals: cwd, env, signal, and fetch come from here.\nexport interface RunContext {\n    stdin:NodeJS.ReadableStream;\n    stdout:NodeJS.WritableStream;\n    stderr:NodeJS.WritableStream;\n    env:Readonly<Record<string, string|undefined>>;\n    cwd:string;\n    signal:AbortSignal;\n    fetch:typeof globalThis.fetch;\n    // The one line reader every prompt in this invocation shares.\n    lines:LineReader;\n    errorFormat:ErrorFormat;\n    // The command tokens that selected this handler, such as\n    // ['gate', 'enable']; empty for bare `roast`.\n    path:string[];\n    // Root help, for commands that print it.\n    rootHelp ():Promise<string>;\n}\n\nexport type CommandArgs = ArgumentsCamelCase<Record<string, unknown>>\n\nexport interface CommandDef {\n    // A yargs command string, such as 'show <id>', or aliases.\n    command:string|readonly string[];\n    // Help description; false hides the command.\n    describe:string|false;\n    // Declares the command's flags and positionals.\n    builder?:(y:Argv) => Argv;\n    // Throws UsageError, ExitSignal, or any other error; never writes\n    // the error itself. A command with subcommands and no handler\n    // prints its help.\n    handler?:(args:CommandArgs, ctx:RunContext) => void|Promise<void>;\n    subcommands?:readonly CommandDef[];\n}\n\nconst registered:CommandDef[] = []\n\n// Adds a command to every later run; returns a function removing it.\nexport function registerCommand (def:CommandDef):() => void {\n    registered.push(def)\n    return () => {\n        const i = registered.indexOf(def)\n        if (i >= 0) registered.splice(i, 1)\n    }\n}\n\nexport function registeredCommands ():readonly CommandDef[] {\n    return registered\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":7,"timestamp":1795227766755},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"4p274PvIeLHllBaEX9PkuO2lnh5cvXgi","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"// The built-in subcommands. Each area adds its CommandDef here\n// (runs, gate, skill, doctor, ignore, trust, init, login, logout).\nimport type { CommandDef } from './command.js'\nimport { gateCommand } from './gate.js'\nimport { ignoreCommand } from './ignore.js'\nimport { reviewCommand } from './review.js'\nimport { skillCommand } from './skill.js'\nimport { trustCommand } from './trust.js'\n\nexport const builtinCommands:readonly CommandDef[] = [\n    reviewCommand,\n    skillCommand,\n    gateCommand,\n    ignoreCommand,\n    trustCommand,\n]\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":6,"timestamp":1795227766755}]}
